		<suppInfo>The DEM in this release is well fit to the source maps and therefore provides an adequate representation of the topography surface prior to the 1980 eruption. Users of these data must be aware of the inherent limitations of source maps and of the methods used to derive these data. Although the digital form of these data removes the constraint imposed by the scale of a paper maps (1:62,500), the detail and accuracy of these data cannot be greater than that of the source maps.
Prior to the 1980 eruption, the most current topographic maps of the area were published in the 1950s at a scale of 1:62,500 with contours derived photogrammetrically from aerial photographs captured in 1952. At this time, dense stands of mature conifer forests encircled the volcano, potentially obscuring view of the bare ground in some places within the extent of the maps that comprise this release. 
The method for creating the DEM uses averaging to reduce 'stair-step' artifacts in areas with shallow slopes or low relief, which commonly occur with interpolation methods taking contour lines as inputs. The resulting DEM is smoother than USGS DEMs of similar resolution and subtle topographic features are not present in many areas and spot elevations specified in the source maps may not be exatly reproduced within this DEM. 
</suppInfo>
		<dataLang><languageCode value="eng" Sync="TRUE"></languageCode><countryCode value="USA" Sync="TRUE"></countryCode></dataLang><dataExt><geoEle><GeoBndBox esriExtentType="search"><exTypeCode Sync="TRUE">1</exTypeCode><westBL Sync="TRUE">-122.476897</westBL><eastBL Sync="TRUE">-122.101840</eastBL><northBL Sync="TRUE">46.332742</northBL><southBL Sync="TRUE">46.153322</southBL></GeoBndBox></geoEle></dataExt></dataIdInfo>
