		<report type="DQQuanAttAcc">
			<measDesc>Absolute accuracy assessment of these data with respect to the ground surface is not possible in areas that were drastically altered by the 1980 eruption. Therefore, accuracy of these data was assessed relative to the source topographic maps.
Relative vertical accuracy was assessed by sampling the DEM at 55,304 points along the digitized contour lines and taking the differences between the expected values (contour elevations) and measured values (DEM elevations). Relative horizontal accuracy was assessed by comparing the locations these points to contours lines derived from the DEM of the same elevations.
The absolute horizontal accuracy standard for USGS topographic maps requires that the positions of 90 percent of all points tested must be accurate within 1/50th of an inch (0.05 centimeters) on the map. For 1:62,500 the source maps, 1/50th of an inch on the map equates to 31.76 m (104.16 ft) on the ground. The absolute vertical accuracy standard for USGS topographic maps require that the elevation of 90 percent of all points tested must be correct within half of the contour interval. Contour interval of the source maps is 80 ft (24.384 m). The measured relative horizontal and relative vertical error of the DEM are within the envelope of error of the absolute accuracy standards required for a 1:62,500 scale USGS topographic map.
</measDesc>
		</report>
