		<dataLineage>
			<prcStep>
				<stepDesc>Digitize topographic maps using ArcGIS Pro to create vectors datasets (a) of 80 ft interval elevation contour lines [Contours_80], (b) stream lines, (c) lake polygons, and (d) elevation points, e.g., mountain peaks. Source maps are scanned paper 1:62,500 USGS topographic maps, and in the summit area, scanned stable mylar bases. Therefore, in the summit region, lines are considered accurate but may not exactly match the scanned paper maps. The scanned paper maps were hand digitized by tracing the contours, lakes, streams, and point elevations. In the summit region, the scanned mylar base was edited to remove all pixels that did not represent contour lines (e.g. text and glacial extents). The Thin tool was used to prepare the image for creating vector lines using the Raster to Polyline tool. Finally, all elevation attributes were added to the contour lines and the topology was verified.</stepDesc>
				<stepDateTm>2023</stepDateTm>
				<stepProc>
					<rpIndName>Joseph A Bard</rpIndName>
					<rpOrgName>U.S. Geological Survey, ALASKA REGION</rpOrgName>
					<rpPosName>Geographer</rpPosName>
					<rpCntInfo>
						<cntPhone>
							<voiceNum>360-993-8982</voiceNum>
						</cntPhone>
						<cntAddress addressType="postal">
							<delPoint>1300 SE Cardinal Court Bldg. 10</delPoint>
							<city>Vancouver</city>
							<adminArea>WA</adminArea>
							<postCode>98683</postCode>
							<country>US</country>
							<eMailAdd>jbard@usgs.gov</eMailAdd>
						</cntAddress>
					</rpCntInfo>
					<role>
						<RoleCd value="009"/>
					</role>
				</stepProc>
			</prcStep>
			<prcStep>
				<stepDesc>Interpolate a DEM [DEM_B] using the Borgefors distance method described in the ILWIS 3.0 User’s Guide, 2001 (see Cross Reference for citation). Following recommendations in the User's Guide, small enclosed contours were created to supplement the digitized contours on hilltops to improve the interpolation in these areas (without these additional contours hilltops may be artificially flattened). </stepDesc>
				<stepDateTm>2023</stepDateTm>
				<stepProc>
					<rpIndName>Joseph A Bard</rpIndName>
					<rpOrgName>U.S. Geological Survey, ALASKA REGION</rpOrgName>
					<rpPosName>Geographer</rpPosName>
					<rpCntInfo>
						<cntPhone>
							<voiceNum>360-993-8982</voiceNum>
						</cntPhone>
						<cntAddress addressType="postal">
							<delPoint>1300 SE Cardinal Court Bldg. 10</delPoint>
							<city>Vancouver</city>
							<adminArea>WA</adminArea>
							<postCode>98683</postCode>
							<country>US</country>
							<eMailAdd>jbard@usgs.gov</eMailAdd>
						</cntAddress>
					</rpCntInfo>
					<role>
						<RoleCd value="009"/>
					</role>
				</stepProc>
			</prcStep>
			<prcStep>
				<stepDesc>Applying a smoothing mean function (ArcGIS Pro Focal Statistics tool, 3x3 window) to DEM_B to create DEM_S. </stepDesc>
				<stepDateTm>2023</stepDateTm>
				<stepProc>
					<rpIndName>Joseph A Bard</rpIndName>
					<rpOrgName>U.S. Geological Survey, ALASKA REGION</rpOrgName>
					<rpPosName>Geographer</rpPosName>
					<rpCntInfo>
						<cntPhone>
							<voiceNum>360-993-8982</voiceNum>
						</cntPhone>
						<cntAddress addressType="postal">
							<delPoint>1300 SE Cardinal Court Bldg. 10</delPoint>
							<city>Vancouver</city>
							<adminArea>WA</adminArea>
							<postCode>98683</postCode>
							<country>US</country>
							<eMailAdd>jbard@usgs.gov</eMailAdd>
						</cntAddress>
					</rpCntInfo>
					<role>
						<RoleCd value="009"/>
					</role>
				</stepProc>
			</prcStep>
			<prcStep>
				<stepDesc>From DEM_S, generate 2 ft interval contour lines [Contours_2ft] using ArcGIS Pro Contour function. The Contour tool's documentation recommends applying a smoothing function to prepare a DEM for contouring.</stepDesc>
				<stepDateTm>2023</stepDateTm>
